  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened support to develop, adopt and implement a National Employment Policy (NEP)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The British Virgin Islands (BVI) is one of the wealthiest islands in the Caribbean, with a GDP per capita of $36,500 in 2020. Despite challenges from the 2017 hurricane and the COVID-19 pandemic, the economy has maintained slow growth, primarily relying on tourism and financial services. The labour market heavily depends on expatriate workers, with over 70% of the labour force being non-nationals. However, prolonged unemployment and high termination rates highlight the need for efficient employment services and improved labour market institutions. The government has pledged to review legislation to enhance inclusivity and facilitate transparent dialogue.

To address these issues, the ILO will support the development of BVI&apos;s National Employment Policy (NEP) in alignment with ILO guidelines. This policy aims to promote economic diversification and inclusion for vulnerable groups, addressing challenges such as climate change, technological progress, and demographic shifts. The NEP will complement the National Sustainable Development Plan adopted in 2023. Key stakeholders include workers, employers, and relevant government counterparts, with the lead counterpart being with the Ministry of Trade, Labour and Financial Services. The ILO will provide technical guidance, support, and facilitate a tripartite structure to guide the diagnostic and formulation process.</narrative>
  </description>
